= Defeat in Cuba

The war is over.

The American expeditionary force has withdrawn from Cuba after failing to force Spain's surrender. Months of fighting, heavy expenditures and thousands of casualties have produced none of the objectives Congress set when the war began.

The fleet returns carrying exhausted veterans instead of victors. Planned celebrations are cancelled. Newspapers that once promised a short campaign now ask who is responsible for the defeat.

Spain has retained control of Cuba. Madrid presents the outcome as a victory and a defense of its remaining Caribbean empire.

The political damage is immediate. Republican leaders who supported the war face attacks in Congress and the press. <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> accuse the administration of wasting American lives on a foreign adventure, while Republicans begin questioning the country's military preparedness.

The War and Navy Departments face demands for reform. Officers begin reviewing the failures of the campaign as the country turns away from the war.

- @peace_terms: Negotiate Peace

@peace_terms

Spain has agreed to end hostilities, but its victory gives Madrid the stronger position at the negotiating table.

American diplomats must now secure the return of prisoners and bring the war to an end. Spain is demanding compensation for the cost of the conflict.

- @pay_indemnity: Pay an indemnity to Spain.
- @white_peace: Negotiate a white peace.

@pay_indemnity
subtitle: End the war at any cost.
choose-if: budget >= 3
unavailable-subtitle: You're Broke
