Spain demands financial compensation for the cost of the war.

The United States agrees to pay an indemnity in exchange for the return of prisoners and a formal end to hostilities. Newspapers condemn the settlement as a national humiliation, while <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> use it as further evidence of Republican incompetence.

The Treasury absorbs the cost. The political damage will last longer.

- @End: Continue

@white_peace
subtitle: End the war without compensation.
on-arrival: {!
    Q.dem_bourbon_strength += 5;
    Q.radical_dissent += 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 2;
!}

After weeks of negotiations, both governments agree to restore the status quo.

Spain retains Cuba and Puerto Rico while the United States abandons its claims arising from the war. No indemnity is paid, but the treaty does little to ease the humiliation of a war fought at great cost with nothing gained.

The Republican administration survives, but its prestige is badly damaged.

- @End: Continue

@End

The nation turns inward.

Debate over military preparedness and overseas intervention returns to Washington. The defeat has also left a simpler question hanging over the Republican Party: who is responsible?

The next election will decide who answers for the war.